# D4.6 - fires only once runner-backup.service has genuinely exhausted its own bounded retries (see
# that unit's own StartLimitIntervalSec/StartLimitBurst/Restart=/RestartSec= - OnFailure= is not
# invoked for a run systemd is about to auto-restart on its own, only for one it has truly given up
# on). A template unit (the trailing "@") so OnFailure=...@%n.service can pass the failed unit's own
# name through as this unit's %i - see runner-backup.service's own header.
#
# Deliberately minimal today, a real review-round finding this repo has no established answer for
# yet: journald alone is not an alert, but this project also has no existing paging/notification
# infrastructure (no paid monitoring service - see docs/DEPLOYMENT_ARCHITECTURE.md's own D4.3.2
# reasoning for why that was deliberately deferred). What this unit actually does is the minimum that
# is genuinely free and already present on any systemd host: log at `crit` priority, distinct from
# ordinary backup output, so `journalctl -p crit` (or any syslog-forwarding the host operator already
# has configured) surfaces it distinctly. Recommended, not yet wired up here: add a second
# `ExecStart=` line below calling a dead-man's-switch ping service (many have a generous free tier -
# absence of a periodic "I'm still working" ping is what actually pages someone) or a webhook curl to
# whatever chat/notification channel the operator already uses day to day - this template unit is
# exactly where either would go.
[Unit]
Description=Alert for a failed %i

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/bin/logger -p daemon.crit -t runner-backup "backup run FAILED and exhausted its retries (%i) - check: journalctl -u runner-backup.service --since today"
